1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,796
Why is Christmas on December twenty-fifth?

2
00:00:05,096 --> 00:00:10,224
Every year, on that day, people around
the world celebrate the birth of Jesus.

3
00:00:10,524 --> 00:00:13,938
And every year, you may
hear the same explanation.

4
00:00:14,238 --> 00:00:18,520
The early Christians, it is said,
simply took over a Roman

5
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:22,576
winter festival, a feast
of the sun, or the Saturnalia.

6
00:00:22,876 --> 00:00:24,744
It is a tidy answer.

7
00:00:25,044 --> 00:00:29,769
But the oldest sources are still here,
and they tell a more careful story.

8
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:35,755
The Gospels describe the birth
in Bethlehem, the shepherds, and the star.

9
00:00:36,055 --> 00:00:38,251
They never give a day or a month.

10
00:00:38,551 --> 00:00:41,670
For about two centuries,
we find no clear sign

11
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:44,650
that Christians kept
the birthday each year.

12
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:48,230
Around the year two hundred,
Clement of Alexandria

13
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:51,711
noted that some people had
tried to work out the day.

14
00:00:52,011 --> 00:00:53,933
He lists several answers.

15
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:57,071
December twenty-fifth is not among them.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,011
The date first appears in a book made
in Rome in the year three fifty-four.

17
00:01:07,311 --> 00:01:11,507
It was an illustrated calendar,
dedicated to a man named

18
00:01:11,507 --> 00:01:15,627
Valentinus, and lettered
by the calligrapher Filocalus.

19
00:01:15,927 --> 00:01:17,837
The original is lost.

20
00:01:18,137 --> 00:01:20,345
We know it through later copies.

21
00:01:21,267 --> 00:01:26,810
One section lists the days on which
the church of Rome remembered its martyrs.

22
00:01:27,110 --> 00:01:31,615
The very first line reads:
eight days before the kalends

23
00:01:31,615 --> 00:01:35,546
of January, Christ is born
in Bethlehem of Judea.

24
00:01:35,846 --> 00:01:39,566
Counted the Roman way,
that is December twenty-fifth.

25
00:01:39,866 --> 00:01:44,246
Many scholars think this list goes
back to about three thirty-six.

26
00:01:44,546 --> 00:01:48,216
The same book also has
the public Roman calendar.

27
00:01:48,516 --> 00:01:54,439
And on the same day, it reads: birthday
of the Unconquered, thirty chariot races.

28
00:01:54,739 --> 00:01:57,119
The word sun is not written there.

29
00:01:58,033 --> 00:02:01,638
So two celebrations share
one date, in one book.

30
00:02:01,938 --> 00:02:03,466
Which came first?

31
00:02:03,766 --> 00:02:06,402
Scholars have offered two main answers.

32
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:10,637
Let us place the evidence
for each, one piece at a time.

33
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:14,213
The first answer begins with the sun.

34
00:02:14,513 --> 00:02:18,296
In two seventy-four,
the emperor Aurelian built

35
00:02:18,296 --> 00:02:22,245
a magnificent temple
in Rome to Sol, the sun god.

36
00:02:22,545 --> 00:02:25,490
Perhaps, the idea goes, the church placed

37
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:28,723
the birth of Christ
on the sun's own festival.

38
00:02:29,023 --> 00:02:30,488
There is support.

39
00:02:30,788 --> 00:02:36,217
A note in the margin of a Syriac
manuscript says that the pagans celebrated

40
00:02:36,217 --> 00:02:41,719
the birthday of the sun on December
twenty-fifth, that Christians joined in,

41
00:02:42,019 --> 00:02:46,671
and so church leaders moved the feast
to that day from January sixth.

42
00:02:46,971 --> 00:02:52,398
And in the four hundreds, Pope Leo
the Great scolded Christians who climbed

43
00:02:52,398 --> 00:02:57,826
the steps of Saint Peter's, then turned
around and bowed to the rising sun.

44
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:02,711
But no one knows who wrote
that margin note, or when.

45
00:03:03,011 --> 00:03:08,416
It was printed in seventeen twenty-one,
and it is not an eyewitness to the choice.

46
00:03:08,716 --> 00:03:12,432
Leo, too, does not say
why the date was chosen.

47
00:03:12,732 --> 00:03:16,749
And no clear source from before
three fifty-four has been

48
00:03:16,749 --> 00:03:21,197
found that puts a festival
of the sun on December twenty-fifth.

49
00:03:21,497 --> 00:03:23,387
What about the Saturnalia?

50
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:27,563
The calendar of three
fifty-four marks it on December

51
00:03:27,563 --> 00:03:31,438
seventeenth, and Macrobius
says it lasted seven days.

52
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:33,788
That ends on the twenty-third.

53
00:03:34,088 --> 00:03:36,819
You may also hear about the god Mithras.

54
00:03:37,119 --> 00:03:39,701
The calendar line does not name him.

55
00:03:40,633 --> 00:03:44,557
The second answer begins not
with a birth, but with a death.

56
00:03:44,857 --> 00:03:49,666
Early Christian writers tried to work
out the day on which Jesus died.

57
00:03:49,966 --> 00:03:54,710
Around the year two hundred, Tertullian
placed it on March twenty-fifth.

58
00:03:55,010 --> 00:04:00,619
Later writers linked that day to the day
his life began, the day he was conceived.

59
00:04:01,533 --> 00:04:05,274
A sermon thought to come
from the three hundreds says it

60
00:04:05,274 --> 00:04:08,879
plainly: our Lord was conceived
on March twenty-fifth.

61
00:04:09,179 --> 00:04:12,333
Nine months later
is December twenty-fifth.

62
00:04:12,633 --> 00:04:16,387
Early in the four hundreds,
Augustine writes it too.

63
00:04:16,687 --> 00:04:20,537
He is believed, Augustine
says, to have been conceived

64
00:04:20,537 --> 00:04:24,605
on March twenty-fifth, the day
on which he also suffered.

65
00:04:24,905 --> 00:04:29,035
And he was born, by tradition,
on December twenty-fifth.

66
00:04:29,967 --> 00:04:32,994
In the east, many churches kept January

67
00:04:32,994 --> 00:04:36,332
sixth as the feast
of his birth and baptism.

68
00:04:36,632 --> 00:04:40,733
Some scholars think a similar
count, from April sixth,

69
00:04:40,733 --> 00:04:44,680
lies behind that date, but
that is a reconstruction.

70
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:50,725
The Armenian Church still celebrates
the birth of Christ on January sixth.

71
00:04:51,025 --> 00:04:53,311
And here, too, there is a gap.

72
00:04:53,611 --> 00:04:59,080
These writers were working after the feast
of December twenty-fifth already existed.

73
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:02,741
Their reasoning may explain
how the date was chosen.

74
00:05:03,041 --> 00:05:06,037
Or it may be a reason given afterwards.

75
00:05:06,967 --> 00:05:09,345
The Roman date travelled east.

76
00:05:09,645 --> 00:05:14,613
In the late three eighties, in the city
of Antioch, John Chrysostom told

77
00:05:14,613 --> 00:05:19,371
his listeners that they had known
this feast for less than ten years.

78
00:05:19,671 --> 00:05:23,579
In the west, he said, people
had known it from long ago.

79
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:28,337
So the oldest record is Roman,
from the three hundreds.

80
00:05:28,637 --> 00:05:32,624
And the two answers are not
quite the same kind of evidence.

81
00:05:32,924 --> 00:05:36,231
The sun gives us
a festival on the same day.

82
00:05:36,531 --> 00:05:41,507
The calculation gives us a reason,
written by Christians themselves.

83
00:05:41,807 --> 00:05:45,264
Neither gives us a record
of the choice being made.

84
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:48,045
There is one more piece.

85
00:05:48,345 --> 00:05:52,332
In the first century, Pliny
the Elder wrote that the winter

86
00:05:52,332 --> 00:05:55,425
solstice falls at about
December twenty-fifth,

87
00:05:55,425 --> 00:05:59,755
and that the spring equinox seems
to fall on March twenty-fifth.

88
00:06:00,055 --> 00:06:03,669
These were calendar markers,
not exact measurements.

89
00:06:03,969 --> 00:06:07,084
But a feast of the sun
would fit at the solstice.

90
00:06:07,384 --> 00:06:11,275
So would a birth counted nine
months from March twenty-fifth.

91
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:15,111
Perhaps the two answers
were never really rivals.

92
00:06:15,411 --> 00:06:18,175
They may point to the same
turning of the year.

93
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:22,353
So, why is Christmas
on December twenty-fifth?

94
00:06:22,653 --> 00:06:26,905
The oldest record we have comes
from Rome, in the three hundreds.

95
00:06:27,205 --> 00:06:31,641
One answer says the date was set
beside a festival of the sun.

96
00:06:31,941 --> 00:06:35,288
The other says it was counted
from March twenty-fifth.

97
00:06:35,588 --> 00:06:38,382
Each has evidence, and each has a gap.

98
00:06:38,682 --> 00:06:41,778
The sources do not let
us choose with certainty.

99
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:45,027
Every story has an origin.

100
00:06:45,327 --> 00:06:48,443
Sometimes, two stories
point to the same day.

101
00:06:49,367 --> 00:06:55,284
This video is based on historical texts
and public museum and library collections.

102
00:06:55,584 --> 00:06:58,304
The narration uses synthetic speech.

103
00:06:58,604 --> 00:07:02,550
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

104
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:05,619
If you enjoyed this story, please like

105
00:07:05,619 --> 00:07:08,971
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

106
00:07:09,271 --> 00:07:11,043
Thank you for watching.
